    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "Classify each number as prime or composite. Show how you know.\n(a) $47$\n(b) $65$",
        "solution_text": "(a) Test factor pairs, as in finding all factors.\n$47\\div2=23$ remainder $1$.\n$47\\div3=15$ remainder $2$.\n$47\\div4=11$ remainder $3$.\n$47\\div5=9$ remainder $2$.\n$47\\div6=7$ remainder $5$.\n$47\\div7=6$ remainder $5$.\nThe possible partners have now passed each other, so there are no new pairs to test.\nThe only positive factors are $1$ and $47$. Therefore $47$ is prime.\n(b) $5\\times13=65$.\nThe positive factors are $1,5,13,65$. There are more than two, so $65$ is composite."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Classify (a) $0$, (b) $1$ and (c) $2$. Explain why being even does not always mean a number is composite.",
        "solution_text": "(a) $0$ is not a positive whole number, so it is neither prime nor composite.\n(b) $1$ has just one positive factor, $1$, so it is neither.\n(c) $2$ has exactly two positive factors, $1$ and $2$, so it is prime.\nAlthough $2$ is even, it is prime. Every even number greater than $2$ has $2$ as a factor as well as $1$ and itself."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "(a) A number has the positive factors $1,3,17,51$. Classify it.\n(b) A student says $25$ is prime because it is odd. Use a factor pair to correct the claim.",
        "solution_text": "(a) There are four distinct positive factors, so the number is composite.\n(b) $5\\times5=25$.\nThe positive factors include $1,5,25$: three distinct factors. Do not count $5$ twice.\nTherefore $25$ is composite, although it is odd."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Which number is prime: $19$, $27$, $35$ or $57$? Explain your choice.",
        "solution_text": "$27=3\\times9$, $35=5\\times7$ and $57=3\\times19$, so these are composite.\nFor $19$, testing $2$, $3$ and $4$ gives a remainder each time. At $5$, the partner is less than $5$, so there are no new pairs.\nThe only positive factors of $19$ are $1$ and $19$. Only $19$ is prime."
      }
    ],
